  "query_preview": "..Another grazing shortwave will keep temperatures on\nthe milder side, relative to the upcoming pattern. Temperatures\ntomorrow will run just a couple of degrees cooler than today.\nThere is little moisture to work with, but an isolated mountain\nshower can't be ruled out during the afternoon with this shortwave\npassage. Otherwise, temperatures start to dramatically warm on\nSunday as a ridge builds into the area. The heat peaks Monday and\nTuesday with temperatures in the upper 80s to low 90s for most\nvalleys and near 100 degrees across lower Washington county.\n\nThere are a couple of scenarios of how the pattern will evolve by\nthe middle of next week. The first scenario (~36% of ensemble\nmembers) has a trough moving into the PNW will shift the ridge \naxis east and bring some cooler temperatures and the chance for \nprecipitation. Moisture does increase ahead of this trough as \nsoutherly flow is enhanced from the ridge to our southeast and \ntrough to our northwest will help pull moisture from the Baja into\nUtah. This moisture will interact with the approaching trough to \ndevelop some convection across the area by the middle of next \nweek. The second scenario (64% of ensemble members) keeps a \ncutoff low off the California coast with mostly dry conditions, \nbut still a slight cooling effect from the trough axis shifting \neast.",
